# Explicit Binding (`call()`, `apply()`, `bind()`)

> **Module:** JavaScript - `this`
>
> **Difficulty:** ⭐⭐⭐⭐☆
>
> **Prerequisites**
>
> - Execution Context
> - `this`
> - Default Binding
> - Implicit Binding
> - Arrow Functions
>
> ---
>
> # Why This Topic Exists
>
> Imagine you have a function:
>
> ```javascript
> function greet() {
>     console.log(this.name);
> }
> ```
>
> And an object:
>
> ```javascript
> const person = {
>     name: "Raj"
> };
> ```
>
> Now you want:
>
> ```javascript
> greet();
> ```
>
> to print:
>
> ```
> Raj
> ```
>
> But…
>
> ```
> There is no object before greet().
> ```
>
> Therefore JavaScript cannot use **Implicit Binding**.
>
> Instead,
>
> ```
> this
> ```
>
> becomes
>
> ```
> undefined
> ```
>
> in strict mode.
>
> So how do we tell JavaScript:
>
> > "Use **this object**."
>
> That's why **Explicit Binding** exists.
>
> ---
>
> # What is Explicit Binding?
>
> Explicit Binding means:
>
> > **The developer manually decides what `this` should be.**
>
> Instead of JavaScript deciding,
>
> **You decide.**
>
> JavaScript simply obeys.
>
> ---
>
> # The Four Ways JavaScript Decides `this`
>
> ```
> Function Call
>        │
>        ▼
> Is it called with new?
>        │
>      Yes
>        ▼
> new Binding
>        │
>      No
>        ▼
> Is call/apply/bind used?
>        │
>      Yes
>        ▼
> Explicit Binding
>        │
>      No
>        ▼
> Is there an object before the function?
>        │
>      Yes
>        ▼
> Implicit Binding
>        │
>      No
>        ▼
> Default Binding
> ```
>
> This decision tree is one of the most important interview concepts.
>
> ---
>
> # First Principles
>
> Functions in JavaScript are **objects**.
>
> Since functions are objects, they contain methods.
>
> Some important methods are:
>
> ```javascript
> Function.prototype.call
> Function.prototype.apply
> Function.prototype.bind
> ```
>
> Every normal JavaScript function automatically inherits these methods.
>
> Example:
>
> ```javascript
> function greet() {}
> ```
>
> Internally:
>
> ```
> greet
>      │
>      ▼
> Function.prototype
>      │
>      ├── call()
>      ├── apply()
>      └── bind()
> ```
>
> That is why every function can use:
>
> ```javascript
> greet.call(...)
> greet.apply(...)
> greet.bind(...)
> ```
>
> ---
>
> # JavaScript Engine Thinking
>
> Consider:
>
> ```javascript
> greet.call(person);
> ```
>
> The engine **does NOT** permanently change the function.
>
> Instead, the engine roughly performs:
>
> ```
> Step 1
>
> Find greet
>
> ↓
>
> Step 2
>
> Temporarily use
>
> this = person
>
> ↓
>
> Step 3
>
> Execute greet
>
> ↓
>
> Step 4
>
> Finish execution
> ```
>
> The original function remains unchanged.
>
> ---
>
> # Visual Explanation
>
> ## Default Binding
>
> ```
> greet()
>
> ↓
>
> this = undefined
> ```
>
> ---
>
> ## Implicit Binding
>
> ```
> person.greet()
>
> ↓
>
> this = person
> ```
>
> ---
>
> ## Explicit Binding
>
> ```
> greet.call(person)
>
> ↓
>
> this = person
> ```
>
> Notice:
>
> We manually selected the object.
>
> ---
>
> # Why Not Simply Pass the Object?
>
> Beginners often ask:
>
> ```javascript
> greet(person);
> ```
>
> Why doesn't this work?
>
> Because:
>
> ```
> Function parameters
> ```
>
> and
>
> ```
> this
> ```
>
> are completely different concepts.
>
> This:
>
> ```javascript
> function greet(person) {
>     console.log(person.name);
> }
> ```
>
> uses an argument.
>
> This:
>
> ```javascript
> function greet() {
>     console.log(this.name);
> }
> ```
>
> uses `this`.
>
> They are not interchangeable.
>
> ---
>
> # When Should You Use Explicit Binding?
>
> Use explicit binding when:
>
> ✅ The call site cannot provide the correct `this`.
>
> ✅ You want to borrow another object's method.
>
> ✅ You are passing callbacks.
>
> ✅ You want predictable behaviour.
>
> ---
>
> # Real World Examples
>
> ### Example 1 — Method Borrowing
>
> ```javascript
> const person1 = {
>     name: "Raj",
>
>     greet() {
>         console.log(this.name);
>     }
> };
>
> const person2 = {
>     name: "John"
> };
>
> person1.greet.call(person2);
> ```
>
> Output:
>
> ```
> John
> ```
>
> We borrowed the method without copying it.
>
> ---
>
> ### Example 2 — Reusing Utility Functions
>
> ```javascript
> function printAge() {
>     console.log(this.age);
> }
>
> const student = {
>     age: 22
> };
>
> printAge.call(student);
> ```
>
> Output:
>
> ```
> 22
> ```
>
> The function is reusable because `this` is supplied explicitly.
>
> ---
>
> # Key Takeaways
>
> - `this` can be controlled manually.
> - `call()`, `apply()`, and `bind()` provide explicit binding.
> - Functions inherit these methods from `Function.prototype`.
> - Explicit binding is checked before implicit binding.
> - The original function is never modified.
> - Understanding the `this` resolution order is a favourite interview topic.
> - Explicit binding enables method borrowing, reusable utilities, and reliable callbacks.
> ```
